# The two asset trees are *content*, not source: git must never rewrite their
# line endings.
#
# `assets/en/**` has a byte-identity contract with the upstream checkout, and two
# gates hash it (`english-byte-identity`, and `translation-freshness` through
# `sourceSha256`). Git for Windows ships `core.autocrlf=true` in the system
# gitconfig, which converts LF to CRLF on checkout. `git status` does not show
# it, because git normalises CRLF back to LF when comparing — so the tree looks
# clean while `pnpm check` fails. Measured on a fresh clone before this file
# existed: 279 of 279 English assets came out CRLF and 264 no longer matched
# upstream.
#
# `-text` keeps the working tree byte-for-byte equal to what upstream ships, which
# is NOT the same as `eol=lf`: `eol=lf` rewrites a CRLF file to LF on checkout,
# which is exactly the transformation this is protecting against. The 279 English
# assets measure 0 CRLF today; the contract must not depend on that staying true.
assets/en/** -text
assets/zh/** -text

# `sync/manifest.json` is the same kind of content: it records the sha256 and the
# byte count of every English asset, and it is the baseline `upstream:check`
# compares against. It was missed when the two asset trees were pinned, so a
# CRLF checkout left the working copy 1784 bytes larger than the committed blob
# while `sameManifest()` normalised the difference away and the script never
# rewrote the file. `sync.mjs` now rewrites it whenever the bytes differ, and this
# line keeps git from producing the difference in the first place.
sync/manifest.json -text
